{"chunk_id": "6520b486cf7e", "text": "In this section of the Mahabharata, the aged Dhritarashtra meets Yudhishthira and inquires carefully about the well\u2011being of the kingdom. His long sequence of questions reflects the ideal duties of a king: protecting subjects, honoring Brahmanas, maintaining justice, safeguarding women and the vulnerable, and keeping the treasury righteous. Yudhishthira replies with concern for Dhritarashtra and the elders living in forest austerity, especially Gandhari and Vidura. Vidura is then seen in a shocking ascetic state\u2014emaciated, naked, and absorbed in severe penance. When Yudhishthira follows him into the forest, Vidura silently transfers his life-force into the king through yogic power and dies standing against a tree. A divine voice reveals Vidura as an embodiment of Dharma itself. The episode deepens Yudhishthira\u2019s moral authority and symbolizes the merging of wisdom and righteousness into the rightful king.\nDhritarashtra questions Yudhishthira about righteous kingship, and the ascetic Vidura mystically transfers his life-force into Yudhishthira through yogic power.\nThemes: righteous kingship, asceticism and renunciation, yogic power, transference of life-force, dharma embodied", "vector": [0.008345534093677998, -0.026223042979836464, 0.004486230202019215, -0.012552007101476192, 0.0029576760716736317, 0.026897158473730087, -0.020991917699575424, -0.021180668845772743, -0.0017425852129235864, -0.01341487281024456, 0.009646574966609478, -0.0015791126061230898, 0.013765412382781506, 0.014439526945352554, -0.019131362438201904, 0.007738831453025341, 0.0465947762131691, -0.009976890869438648, 0.038667190819978714, -0.026425277814269066, -0.004179507959634066, -0.016529280692338943, 0.0028818382415920496, 0.025805093348026276, -0.012363255023956299, 0.028366725891828537, 0.01946841925382614, -0.00903313048183918, -0.00043985951924696565, -0.016771961003541946, -0.012369995936751366, -0.003768298542127013, 0.0089387549087